WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.109
Ranger Holter felt the bark of the tree trunk
slamming into his back, practically knocking

00:00:05.109 --> 00:00:10.349
all the air out of his lungs as he tried desperately
to slow his frantic breathing.

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:15.540
The sound of his own panting filled him with
a sudden horrifying thought: if he could hear

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.730
his own ragged breaths, then it could hear
them too.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.960
He clamped a hand over his mouth, clenching
his jaw shut so tightly that he thought his

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.189
teeth might splinter as he pushed them together.

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:32.700
His free hand gripped the tranquilizer gun,
shuddering as the snap of a twig rang out

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.219
somewhere behind him.

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:35.930
It had caught up to him already.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.930
He could bolt, make a quick dash for cover
behind another tree, but it was close enough

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.610
now that it would see him.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:48.550
And if it did, it would come rushing after
him with a speed that even a seasoned Park

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.050
Ranger couldn’t match.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.500
As much as he tried to force himself to stay
still, the petrified curiosity got the better

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.500
of him.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.010
Holter peeked out from behind the tree, and
immediately wished he hadn’t.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:05.180
It had all started out as what sounded like
an everyday occurrence.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.830
High atop his watchtower in the Glacier National
Park, Ranger Holter had been enjoying the

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:15.140
view of Montana’s Rocky Mountains, a cup
of coffee from his thermos in his hand.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.840
Then the familiar squawk of his radio had
cut short his appreciation of the serene,

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:20.840
peaceful scenery.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:25.390
Holter sighed, tossing the remaining contents
of the cup over the tower’s railing, wondering

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.509
what kind of day he was in for as he went
to answer the radio.

00:01:29.509 --> 00:01:32.890
Most of the time, things in Glacier Park were
as peaceful as they looked.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:36.930
But the Park Ranger knew that staying vigilant
could make all the difference when it came

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:41.259
to finding a missing hiker in time, or the
difference between deterring a group of campers

00:01:41.259 --> 00:01:45.940
away from a family of bears and having a hell
of a mess to clean up.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.659
He pushed down on the button of the speaker
microphone, connected to the bulky radio that

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:52.120
took up practically an entire desk.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.350
“Holter, thank god!”

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.130
came the relieved voice from the other side.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.470
“We’ve been trying to reach everyone,
put them on alert!

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:00.869
We’ve got trouble, reports of a coyote gone
feral attacking folks!”

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:04.700
“I’m on it,” Holter replied, grabbing
his gear from the cramped watchtower as he

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.219
continued to speak into the mic.

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:07.939
“Where did the report come in from?”

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:11.280
“Not far from your station, I’d say about
a klick west from your position.”

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.970
Stowing his tranquilizer gun, Ranger Holder
descended the ladder from his watchtower down

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:16.970
to ground level.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:22.480
As a Park Ranger, Holder valued the environment
he worked in and As a fan of the SCP universe,

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.480
we know how much you value intricate world-building,
or in some cases, world destroying.

